<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <PropertyGroup>
    <TargetFrameworks>net8.0;net9.0;net10.0</TargetFrameworks>
    <LangVersion>latest</LangVersion>
    <ImplicitUsings>enable</ImplicitUsings>
    <Nullable>enable</Nullable>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <PropertyGroup Label="NugetProperties">
    <Authors>Kebechet</Authors>
    <IsPackable>true</IsPackable>
    <PackageId>Kebechet.Api.ToMcp</PackageId>
    <Description>A C# source generator that transforms ASP.NET Core API endpoints into MCP (Model Context Protocol) tools.</Description>
    <RepositoryUrl>https://github.com/Kebechet/Api.ToMcp</RepositoryUrl>
    <PackageProjectUrl>https://github.com/Kebechet/Api.ToMcp</PackageProjectUrl>
    <PackageReadmeFile>README.md</PackageReadmeFile>
    <PackageLicenseFile>LICENSE</PackageLicenseFile>
    <RepositoryType>git</RepositoryType>
    <GeneratePackageOnBuild>True</GeneratePackageOnBuild>
    <DebugSymbols>true</DebugSymbols>
    <DebugType>embedded</DebugType>
    <PackageIcon>icon.png</PackageIcon>
    <PackageTags>mcp;model-context-protocol;source-generator;aspnetcore;api;claude;ai</PackageTags>
    <Version>3.0.0</Version>
    <PackageReleaseNotes>
Changed:
- ⚠️ A tool's API request always runs in process, through the app's own request pipeline, and the package sends nothing over the network. McpScopeOptions.AreApiCallsInProcess is removed: delete it from AddMcpTools. The README's "Migrating from 2.x" says what to do for each reason an app called over HTTP.
- ⚠️ McpHttpInvoker's public constructor, which built an invoker that called over HTTP, is removed. Resolve IMcpHttpInvoker from the app's services instead.
- ⚠️ services.AddHttpClient&lt;IMcpHttpInvoker, McpHttpInvoker&gt;(), with which tests handed the invoker a TestServer handler, now makes every tool call fail with an InvalidOperationException that says to remove it. Remove it: under WebApplicationFactory the tools run in process.
- ⚠️ The invoker's HttpClient gets a primary handler that refuses every request, so no request of it leaves the process. A primary handler the app sets with ConfigurePrimaryHttpMessageHandler is never called, and a request app code sends through that client is served by the app in process. Remove such a handler.
- ⚠️ A tool that called another server through McpTools:BaseUrl now calls the app itself. Register an IMcpHttpInvoker of your own for that server, as the README shows.
- ⚠️ McpTools:BaseUrl and ISelfBaseUrlProvider now only name a tool's request that has no MCP request to take its address from, such as one run outside an MCP request; that address is never called. The tool's request no longer carries X-Forwarded-Proto, and DefaultBaseUrlProvider lost its constructor that took an IHttpContextAccessor, its error for an http:// binding off loopback and its preference for a loopback binding. Behind a TLS-terminating proxy, McpTools:BaseUrl and loopback among the KnownProxies are no longer needed.
- ⚠️ A tool called in a host that has not built the app's request pipeline fails with an InvalidOperationException that says why, and a host that starts without building it logs an error at startup.
- ⚠️ An action that requires a traceparent, tracestate, baggage, Correlation-Context or X-Forwarded-Proto header gets MCP009 and no tool, as the tool's request never carries one. Make the parameter optional.
- ⚠️ The message handlers and DefaultRequestHeaders of the invoker's named HttpClient (IMcpHttpInvoker), which 2.x ran only over HTTP, now apply to every tool's request: AddHttpMessageHandler, AddStandardResilienceHandler with its retries and timeouts, AddLogger, the handlers ConfigureHttpClientDefaults gives every client, as .NET Aspire's service defaults give AddStandardResilienceHandler, and the client factory's logging. The request passes them all before it is served in process. A retrying handler runs a request that failed with a transient status, such as 503, through the app again, whatever its method. To keep the resilience handlers of ConfigureHttpClientDefaults off the tool's request, call services.AddHttpClient(nameof(IMcpHttpInvoker)).RemoveAllResilienceHandlers() (Microsoft.Extensions.Http.Resilience 9.2.0 or later, experimental: EXTEXP0001). A default header never replaces Authorization, X-MCP-Internal-Call or a [FromHeader] argument. The client's Timeout applies as before.
- ⚠️ Tool calls now show in the app's request metrics, spans and request log: request rates, latency percentiles and error rates on dashboards and alerts include each tool's API request. Filter on api_to_mcp.tool_request to leave them out.
- The log entries of the tool's request carry a SpanId of their own, with the tool call's span as their ParentId, as does the traceId of its ProblemDetails; before, they carried the tool call's. Their RequestPath includes the path base, as hosting's does, and their scope exists only while hosting's log category is on.
- A canceled tool call's request is logged and measured with the status 499 when its response had not started, as Kestrel records a client that went away, and its cancellation is no error.
- ⚠️ A tool call whose arguments make a request line or headers over Kestrel's limits, such as a long query string or [FromHeader] value, now gets that error result instead of reaching the API. Raise the limit in ConfigureKestrel, or send the value in the body.
- ⚠️ The tool's request body is held to the server's MaxRequestBodySize, 30,000,000 bytes by default under Kestrel, IIS and HTTP.sys, instead of no limit. A larger body gets HTTP 413 unless the action's [RequestSizeLimit] or [DisableRequestSizeLimit] lifts it.
- The tool's request keeps the percent-encoding it was sent with in its RawTarget, such as /users/a%40b, instead of one re-encoded from the decoded path.

Added:
- IMcpToolRequestFeature and HttpContext.IsMcpToolRequest(). A tool's request carries the feature, which no client can send, unlike X-MCP-Internal-Call, and its ToolName names the tool whose call sent the request. A rate limiter's partition, a middleware or a logger can tell a tool's request from a client's by it. The README shows a rate limiter that gives the tool's requests a partition of their own: they carry the MCP request's connection, so a per-address limiter counted them against the client that still holds the MCP request.
- The tool's API request gets the logs, the span and the metrics ASP.NET Core's hosting gives a request the server receives, through hosting's own instruments: Request starting and Request finished in Microsoft.AspNetCore.Hosting.Diagnostics, under the RequestId and RequestPath scope; the server span Microsoft.AspNetCore.Hosting.HttpRequestIn, a child of the tool call's, with the DiagnosticListener events that OpenTelemetry's ASP.NET Core instrumentation and Application Insights read; and http.server.request.duration and http.server.active_requests in the meter Microsoft.AspNetCore.Hosting, with http.route, error.type and the tags of IHttpMetricsTagsFeature. AddAspNetCoreInstrumentation() records it with no change. The span, the duration and the log scope carry api_to_mcp.tool_request = true. Hosting's EventSource counters, such as total-requests, do not count it.
- A tool's request is held to the request limits of the server the app runs on, as the same request sent to the server is. Under Kestrel, a request line over MaxRequestLineSize (8 KB) gets HTTP 414: URI Too Long, and headers over MaxRequestHeadersTotalSize (32 KB) or MaxRequestHeaderCount (100) get HTTP 431: Request Header Fields Too Large, before the API sees the request. IIS's request filtering and HTTP.sys's registry limits, which hold a request outside the app, do not apply, and TestServer has no limits.

Fixed:
- The MCP endpoint that MapMcpEndpoint maps refuses a tool's request without UseMcpLoopPrevention too (https://github.com/Kebechet/Api.ToMcp/issues/100). Before, an app without the middleware served such a request as a new MCP request inside the tool call. The middleware is still needed for an endpoint mapped with the SDK's MapMcp.
    </PackageReleaseNotes>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <FrameworkReference Include="Microsoft.AspNetCore.App" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!-- ModelContextProtocol.AspNetCore as direct dependency for consumers -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageReference Include="ModelContextProtocol.AspNetCore" Version="2.2.0" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!-- Reference Runtime (which transitively references Abstractions) - bundled in package -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="..\Api.ToMcp.Runtime\Api.ToMcp.Runtime.csproj" PrivateAssets="all" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!-- Include Generator as analyzer for development -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="..\Api.ToMcp.Generator\Api.ToMcp.Generator.csproj"
                      OutputItemType="Analyzer"
                      ReferenceOutputAssembly="false"
                      PrivateAssets="all" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!-- Include Generator DLL as analyzer in the NuGet package -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <None Include="..\Api.ToMcp.Generator\bin\$(Configuration)\netstandard2.0\Api.ToMcp.Generator.dll"
          Pack="true"
          PackagePath="analyzers/dotnet/cs"
          Visible="false" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!-- Package README, LICENSE, and icon -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <None Include="..\..\README.md">
      <Pack>True</Pack>
      <PackagePath>\</PackagePath>
    </None>
    <None Include="..\..\LICENSE">
      <Pack>True</Pack>
      <PackagePath>\</PackagePath>
    </None>
    <None Include="..\..\icon.png" Condition="Exists('..\..\icon.png')">
      <Pack>True</Pack>
      <PackagePath>\</PackagePath>
    </None>
  </ItemGroup>

  <!-- Include build targets. Deliberately NO buildTransitive: the generator.json
       auto-creation must not flow to transitive consumers (e.g. test projects that
       reference the API project). See https://github.com/Kebechet/Api.ToMcp/issues/3 -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <None Include="build\**\*" Pack="true" PackagePath="build" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!-- Automatically include Runtime and Abstractions DLLs in the package -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <TargetsForTfmSpecificBuildOutput>$(TargetsForTfmSpecificBuildOutput);IncludeBundledAssemblies</TargetsForTfmSpecificBuildOutput>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <Target Name="IncludeBundledAssemblies">
    <ItemGroup>
      <BuildOutputInPackage Include="$(OutputPath)Api.ToMcp.Runtime.dll" />
      <BuildOutputInPackage Include="$(OutputPath)Api.ToMcp.Runtime.xml" />
      <BuildOutputInPackage Include="$(OutputPath)Api.ToMcp.Abstractions.dll" />
      <BuildOutputInPackage Include="$(OutputPath)Api.ToMcp.Abstractions.xml" />
    </ItemGroup>
  </Target>

</Project>
